{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794546201997,"sessionID":"ses_e2cb7b3d5ffeOOZ1uunf8eAcfG","part":{"partID":"prt_1d349f3a5001uoZ7SCA5QoOH2R","sessionID":"ses_e2cb7b3d5ffeOOZ1uunf8eAcfG","messageID":"msg_1d349e62a001wf9Ow84WO0j1dU","type":"tool","id":"call_6eeccfa5424145b1bf79a0d1","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/INSTALL-SKILLED.md","offset":110,"limit":80},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/INSTALL-SKILLED.md, lines 110-189\n110: ### Git Hooks\n111: \n112: The first AI session installs the repository's git hooks. The root README's [Git Hooks](README.md#git-hooks) section says what each hook blocks. `/doctor:git setup` checks the hooks and the sk-git settings.\n113: \n114: Your checkout is ready once `/doctor:setup` passes. Run `/doctor:setup` again whenever something looks wrong. To use Skilled in a project of your own, pick a way in below.\n115: \n116: ---\n117: \n118: ## 3. LINK IT INTO YOUR PROJECT\n119: \n120: ### Step 1: Run the Install Guide\n121: \n122: Open your Skilled checkout in your runtime and run:\n123: \n124: ```\n125: /doctor:install\n126: ```\n127: \n128: It asks four questions in one prompt:\n129: \n130: 1. **Target repo:** the absolute path of your project.\n131: 2. **Runtimes:** the AI coding tools you use. Pick any of `claude`, `opencode`, `codex`, `cursor`, `devin` and `pi`.\n132: 3. **Link shape:** choose `auto`. [What Gets Linked](#what-gets-linked) explains the shapes.\n133: 4. **Instruction file:** `embed` adds the shipped sections to your project's own `AGENTS.md`. `link` points `AGENTS.md`, `CLAUDE.md` and `REPO RULES.md` at the checkout's copies.\n134: \n135: <!-- readme-check\n136: allow-ref CLAUDE.md | the instruction file in the operator's target repo, which this checkout does not ship\n137: -->\n138: \n139: The guide writes nothing. It prints the steps below with your paths filled in.\n140: \n141: ### Step 2: Run the Printed Steps\n142: \n143: | Step | Command | Run it from | What it does |\n144: | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n145: | 1 | `/doctor:symlink link --root <dir> --runtimes <list> --mode auto` | the checkout | Links `.skilled` and your runtime folders into the project |\n146: | 2 | `/doctor:agents-md embed --root <dir>` | the checkout | Adds the shipped sections to your `AGENTS.md`. Skip it if you chose `link` |\n147: | 3 | `/doctor:mcp install` | the checkout | Builds and registers Code Mode, the bridge your runtime uses to call MCP tools |\n148: | 4 | `/doctor:git setup` | your project | Checks the git hooks and sk-git settings. Run `/doctor:git standards` afterwards to write repo-owned commit, PR and branch rules; until then the shipped defaults apply |\n149: | 5 | `/doctor:skill-advisor embedder` | your project | Sets up meaning matching for commands. It shows the cost and asks before it pulls the model or starts the server |\n150: | 6 | `/doctor:setup` | your project | Confirms the whole setup works |\n151: \n152: If you chose `link` for the instruction file, step 1 also carries `--link-instructions` and step 2 drops out.\n153: \n154: ### Step 3: Check the Links\n155: \n156: ```\n157: /doctor:symlink check --root <dir>\n158: ```\n159: \n160: Each folder should read `linked-whole` or `linked-merge`. A folder that reads `partial-merge` lacks entries the checkout gained since your last `link`. Run `link` again to add them.\n161: \n162: ### What Gets Linked\n163: \n164: - **Always:** `.skilled`.\n165: - **Per runtime:** `.claude` with `.mcp.json`, `.opencode`, `.codex`, `.cursor`, `.devin` or `.pi`.\n166: - **On request:** `--include` takes `repo-rules`, `github`, `utcp` or `env`. They add the `repo-rules` folder, `.github`, `.utcp_config.json` and `.env`.\n167: - **Secrets:** linking `.env` shares the checkout's secrets with every tool in the project. The plan warns you before you approve it.\n168: \n169: The link shape decides how each folder is linked:\n170: \n171: - **`whole`:** each folder becomes one link to the checkout where it can.\n172: - **`merge`:** the folder stays a real folder, and each entry inside it becomes its own link.\n173: - **`auto`:** picks per folder, as described below.\n174: \n175: With `auto`, a folder your project already has stays a real folder. Each entry inside it becomes its own link, and your own files there stay untouched.\n176: \n177: A folder your project lacks becomes one link. The exception is a folder that holds a link to a sibling folder, such as `.skilled` (its `specs` link) or `.claude` (its `skills` link). The engine builds that folder entry by entry instead.\n178: \n179: ### Keep the Links Out of Git\n180: \n181: Most links point at your machine's checkout, so never commit them. `link` writes an ignore line for each of those to `.git/info/exclude`. Git reads that file but never commits it. Pass `--ignore gitignore` to write `.gitignore` instead.\n182: \n183: One link is different. When your project has its own `specs/` folder, `.skilled/specs` links to it with a relative path. That link works on any machine, so it receives no ignore line and you can commit it.\n184: \n185: ### Undo a Link\n186: \n187: Each `link` run ends by printing its undo command:\n188: \n189: ```\n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 190]","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":true},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/INSTALL-SKILLED.md, lines 110-189\n110: ### Git Hooks\n111: \n112: The first AI session installs the repository's git hooks. The root README's [Git Hooks](README.md#git-hooks) section says what each hook blocks. `/doctor:git setup` checks the hooks and the sk-git settings.\n113: \n114: Your checkout is ready once `/doctor:setup` passes. Run `/doctor:setup` again whenever something looks wrong. To use Skilled in a project of your own, pick a way in below.\n115: \n116: ---\n117: \n118: ## 3. LINK IT INTO YOUR PROJECT\n119: \n120: ### Step 1: Run the Install Guide\n121: \n122: Open your Skilled checkout in your runtime and run:\n123: \n124: ```\n125: /doctor:install\n126: ```\n127: \n128: It asks four questions in one prompt:\n129: \n130: 1. **Target repo:** the absolute path of your project.\n131: 2. **Runtimes:** the AI coding tools you use. Pick any of `claude`, `opencode`, `codex`, `cursor`, `devin` and `pi`.\n132: 3. **Link shape:** choose `auto`. [What Gets Linked](#what-gets-linked) explains the shapes.\n133: 4. **Instruction file:** `embed` adds the shipped sections to your project's own `AGENTS.md`. `link` points `AGENTS.md`, `CLAUDE.md` and `REPO RULES.md` at the checkout's copies.\n134: \n135: <!-- readme-check\n136: allow-ref CLAUDE.md | the instruction file in the operator's target repo, which this checkout does not ship\n137: -->\n138: \n139: The guide writes nothing. It prints the steps below with your paths filled in.\n140: \n141: ### Step 2: Run the Printed Steps\n142: \n143: | Step | Command | Run it from | What it does |\n144: | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n145: | 1 | `/doctor:symlink link --root <dir> --runtimes <list> --mode auto` | the checkout | Links `.skilled` and your runtime folders into the project |\n146: | 2 | `/doctor:agents-md embed --root <dir>` | the checkout | Adds the shipped sections to your `AGENTS.md`. Skip it if you chose `link` |\n147: | 3 | `/doctor:mcp install` | the checkout | Builds and registers Code Mode, the bridge your runtime uses to call MCP tools |\n148: | 4 | `/doctor:git setup` | your project | Checks the git hooks and sk-git settings. Run `/doctor:git standards` afterwards to write repo-owned commit, PR and branch rules; until then the shipped defaults apply |\n149: | 5 | `/doctor:skill-advisor embedder` | your project | Sets up meaning matching for commands. It shows the cost and asks before it pulls the model or starts the server |\n150: | 6 | `/doctor:setup` | your project | Confirms the whole setup works |\n151: \n152: If you chose `link` for the instruction file, step 1 also carries `--link-instructions` and step 2 drops out.\n153: \n154: ### Step 3: Check the Links\n155: \n156: ```\n157: /doctor:symlink check --root <dir>\n158: ```\n159: \n160: Each folder should read `linked-whole` or `linked-merge`. A folder that reads `partial-merge` lacks entries the checkout gained since your last `link`. Run `link` again to add them.\n161: \n162: ### What Gets Linked\n163: \n164: - **Always:** `.skilled`.\n165: - **Per runtime:** `.claude` with `.mcp.json`, `.opencode`, `.codex`, `.cursor`, `.devin` or `.pi`.\n166: - **On request:** `--include` takes `repo-rules`, `github`, `utcp` or `env`. They add the `repo-rules` folder, `.github`, `.utcp_config.json` and `.env`.\n167: - **Secrets:** linking `.env` shares the checkout's secrets with every tool in the project. The plan warns you before you approve it.\n168: \n169: The link shape decides how each folder is linked:\n170: \n171: - **`whole`:** each folder becomes one link to the checkout where it can.\n172: - **`merge`:** the folder stays a real folder, and each entry inside it becomes its own link.\n173: - **`auto`:** picks per folder, as described below.\n174: \n175: With `auto`, a folder your project already has stays a real folder. Each entry inside it becomes its own link, and your own files there stay untouched.\n176: \n177: A folder your project lacks becomes one link. The exception is a folder that holds a link to a sibling folder, such as `.skilled` (its `specs` link) or `.claude` (its `skills` link). The engine builds that folder entry by entry instead.\n178: \n179: ### Keep the Links Out of Git\n180: \n181: Most links point at your machine's checkout, so never commit them. `link` writes an ignore line for each of those to `.git/info/exclude`. Git reads that file but never commits it. Pass `--ignore gitignore` to write `.gitignore` instead.\n182: \n183: One link is different. When your project has its own `specs/` folder, `.skilled/specs` links to it with a relative path. That link works on any machine, so it receives no ignore line and you can commit it.\n184: \n185: ### Undo a Link\n186: \n187: Each `link` run ends by printing its undo command:\n188: \n189: ```\n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 190]"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/INSTALL-SKILLED.md\",\"offset\":110,\"limit\":80}"},"time":{"start":1794546201509,"end":1794546201997}}}}
